<blockquote>
<p><strong>Journal entry-Dr. Todd-date (𶐉𶐉𶐉𶐉𶐿𶐉)</strong><br />
I required an assistant, I had Edward and Jim working, but I need someone, someone with the same experiences as me. That’s why I persuaded Jacqe to hire him.<br />
On our exploit, the equipment informed us of the size of the cavity, the statistics suggest that the total length would be around that of the Manhattan island, as it would be as high as at least 200 meters!<br />
The equipment also indicated water and flora.<br />
End journal entry</p>
</blockquote>
<blockquote>
<p><strong>Journal entry-Dr. Dravis-date(𶐉𶐉𶐉𶐉𶐿𶐉)</strong><br />
Must be fungi, I thought in the morning after thinking about it overnight, which nonetheless made me more excited about the idea that the cave may be pent up with methane as I write!<br />
It’s only a thought but there’s a high chance that it might be. Minutes later I went to Todd’s and told him about my theory about the cave having fungi, Todd exited with my theory called Jim, and we went to have lunch at Edward’s. there we stayed speculating, theorizing, and talking about the cavity and its contents.<br />
Three days later we concluded, that at some point in time it was a subterranean river, bringing spores underneath through the river, the spores grow.<br />
And as the cave grew in size, the fungi grew in numbers until such time it became self-sustaining. The mushrooms might have thrived to such extent that they evolved in the lack of light so when the river was enclosed the contents remained self-sustaining, the dead fungi released methane and fungi drew on the dead fungi and that’s how it stayed for years, until now.<br />
Our plan was simple we drill a hole in the roof of the cavity, a plant above will suck the theorized methane, convert it through a series of complex machinery that turns the methane into electricity for the years to come, backup generators will be plugged if the output electricity will be too excessive.<br />
We had planned this for months now.<br />
It will work.<br />
It should have. We checked the machine, nothing was wrong, we decided to check the pipes.<br />
Turns out the methane in the cave was not enough to power the generators, we decided to turn off the plant, jacqe enraged by this demanded that we would check the cave if anything in there might still be of use. Three days later, so here I am writing my journal, a few moments later Todd comes in and tells me that the equipment is ready.<br />
End journal entry</p>
</blockquote>
